8¾¹˜ÀyªKÅ<áuÛðe›\ÚÏŸ¼v>VÖahŽZ{"rendered":{"html":"<p>GCSL evaluation uses reference-counted values and explicit execution\ncontexts. A closure captures values; a frame holds the current call's\nslots. This supports value semantics without exposing shared mutable\nvariables to programs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"evaluation-state--futures-and-memo-tables\"><span class=\"heading-number\">200.1</span> Futures and memo tables <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#evaluation-state--futures-and-memo-tables\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A future begins pending, becomes running during evaluation, and records a\nsuccessful result. Reentering running work is an error rather than an\ninfinite cycle through the same pending computation. A failed evaluation\ndoes not become a cached success.</p>\n<p>Memo tables belong to the execution context and are keyed by closure\nidentity and argument values, with supported ambient inputs included when\nused. Keeping tables outside closures avoids introducing reference cycles\nthrough cached keys and results. Successful entries for live closures\nremain until their context ends; they are not an eviction-based cache.</p>\n<h2 id=\"evaluation-state--exits-and-cleanup\"><span class=\"heading-number\">200.2</span> Exits and cleanup <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#evaluation-state--exits-and-cleanup\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Runtime errors and language control flow share an unwinding path through\nthe evaluator. A loop handles its own <code>break</code> or <code>continue</code>; a function,\nfuture, or labeled block handles a matching <code>return</code>. Intermediate\noperations restore stack and evaluation state before propagating an exit.</p>\n<p>A failed REPL input does not commit its new bindings or memo-table changes\nto the session. Effects already performed outside the interpreter are not\nrolled back. A file written or a subprocess started before an error still\nrequires the host's ordinary handling.</p>\n<p>A workspace remembers the first runtime failure of each file. A later\nrequest that needs that file receives the original diagnostic without\nrerunning its effects or starting dependent files. Successful prior files\nkeep their values, and unrelated files can still run. Create a new\nworkspace object when you intentionally need to retry evaluation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"evaluation-state--resource-limits\"><span class=\"heading-number\">200.3</span> Resource limits <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#evaluation-state--resource-limits\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>The parser's maximum expression depth is <code>gcsl::MAX_DEPTH</code>, 10,000 levels.\nRuntime expressions, calls, and awaits share a nesting budget; calls cost\nadditional levels. Iterations reuse their body's depth rather than adding\none level per iteration. Tail calls are not eliminated.</p>\n<p>These limits protect the recursive implementation, not total computation.\nA program can still loop indefinitely, allocate large values, capture\nlarge process output, or retain many memoized results. The embedding host\nowns any execution timeout and memory limit.</p>\n<p>The main implementation lives in <code>eval.rs</code>, <code>value.rs</code>, and <code>session.rs</code>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"evaluation-state--futures-and-memo-tables","title":"Futures and memo tables","number":"200.1"},{"level":2,"id":"evaluation-state--exits-and-cleanup","title":"Exits and cleanup","number":"200.2"},{"level":2,"id":"evaluation-state--resource-limits","title":"Resource limits","number":"200.3"}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[],"anchors":[]},"refs":null}